Drug-Induced Hepatotoxicity (DILI) caused by an anti-tuberculosis regimen.

### Patient Demographics
* Identifier: Patient A.P.
* Age: 45 years
* Gender: Female
* Weight: 55 kg
* Medical History: Pulmonary Tuberculosis (newly diagnosed), no baseline liver dysfunction, non-smoker, non-drinker.

### Clinical Chronology
* Month 1 (Day 1): Initiated on standard fixed-dose combination anti-TB therapy containing Isoniazid 300 mg, Rifampicin 600 mg, Pyrazinamide 1500 mg, and Ethambutol 1000 mg daily.
* Month 1 (Day 26): Patient developed right upper quadrant abdominal pain, anorexia, severe nausea, dark urine, and progressive scleral icterus.
* Laboratory Findings at Admission (Day 28):
  * Alanine Aminotransferase (ALT): 620 U/L (Baseline: 22 U/L; elevated to greater than 10 times upper limit of normal)
  * Aspartate Aminotransferase (AST): 540 U/L (Baseline: 25 U/L)
  * Total Bilirubin: 4.2 mg/dL (Baseline: 0.8 mg/dL)
  * Viral Hepatitis Serology (Hep A, B, C, E): Negative
  * Abdominal Ultrasound: Normal biliary tract, no gallstones or ductal dilation.
* Intervention: Immediate discontinuation of all hepatotoxic anti-TB drugs (Isoniazid, Rifampicin, Pyrazinamide); patient managed with supportive care and non-hepatotoxic background regimen (Ethambutol and Levofloxacin).
* Follow-up (Day 21 post-discontinuation): Complete resolution of jaundice and abdominal pain; ALT/AST normalized to under 40 U/L and total bilirubin returned to 0.9 mg/dL.

